<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=containerization">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Containerisation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1766">1766</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Intermodal containers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Containerisation is a system of freight transport using standardised containers that can be moved between ships, trains and trucks without being opened. It began in English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining regions: in 1766 James Brindley designed the 'starvationer' box boat, with ten wooden containers, to carry coal from Worsley Delph to Manchester by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridgewater-canal">Bridgewater Canal</a>. In 1955 Malcom McLean and Keith Tantlinger developed the modern intermodal container, and on 26 April 1956 McLean's SS Ideal X carried 58 trailer vans from Newark to Houston. ISO 668 set standard terminology and dimensions in January 1968.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/containerization.jpg" alt="Containerisation" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Captain Albert E. Theberge, NOAA Corps (ret.) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Line3174_-_Shipping_Containers_at_the_terminal_at_Port_Elizabeth,_New_Jersey_-_NOAA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1766</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Bridgewater Canal, England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>James Brindley · Malcom McLean · Keith Tantlinger · <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The modern intermodal container dates from 1955 to 1956. The first container ship, the Autocarrier, was launched in February 1931, and 'loose boxes' for coal from the late 1780s.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/crane-machine">Crane<span>late 6th century BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=containerization">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
